@*
    Every screen announces where you are the same way: a section eyebrow that goes back, the
    thing's own name as the page's one <h1>, and the actions that belong to it.

    It replaces a toolbar that carried four different jobs in one flat row — a back link, the
    entity name, a cross-link and a primary button — where the name read as another button and sat
    off the others' baseline because it was set in mono at a title size.

    The actions are two slots rather than one, and that is the ordinary toolbar behaviour: a screen
    names its primary action and its secondary ones separately, and this decides at the breakpoint
    which are shown. Above 720 px they all sit on the row; below it the secondaries fold into an
    overflow menu, because a header row of equal-weight buttons on a phone is a wall rather than a
    toolbar, and it gets worse with every control a screen gains.

    One markup tree and one media query — not two components — for the reason the shell gives about
    a second navigation. The secondary fragment is rendered twice, inline and in the sheet, and CSS
    decides which of the two is on the screen; the overflow menu reuses `Sheet`, so it inherits the
    scrim, Escape, focus and scroll lock rather than reimplementing four behaviours nobody notices
    until one is missing.
*@

<header class="a-pagehead">
    @if (Eyebrow is { Length: > 0 })
    {
        @if (EyebrowHref is { Length: > 0 })
        {
            <a class="a-pagehead__eyebrow" href="@EyebrowHref">@Eyebrow</a>
        }
        else
        {
            <span class="a-pagehead__eyebrow">@Eyebrow</span>
        }
    }

    <div class="a-pagehead__row">
        <h1 class="@($"a-page-title{(Mono ? " a-mono" : string.Empty)}")">@Title</h1>
        @if (Primary is not null || Secondary is not null)
        {
            <div class="a-pagehead__actions">
                @if (Secondary is not null)
                {
                    @* display:contents above the breakpoint, display:none below it, so these keep
                       sitting in the actions row itself rather than in a box of their own. *@
                    <div class="a-pagehead__secondary">
                        @Secondary
                    </div>

                    <button type="button"
                            class="a-btn a-btn--ghost a-btn--sm a-only-mobile a-pagehead__more"
                            @onclick="() => _overflow = true" aria-label="More actions"
                            data-testid="pagehead-overflow"
                            aria-expanded="@(_overflow ? "true" : "false")">
                        <Icon Path="@Icons.More" />
                    </button>
                }

                @Primary
            </div>
        }
    </div>

    @if (Subtitle is { Length: > 0 })
    {
        <p class="a-pagehead__sub">@Subtitle</p>
    }
</header>

@*
    The same fragment again. Closing on a click inside is deliberate: every control that folds in
    here either navigates or opens something, so a menu that stayed open would sit over what the
    operator just asked for.
*@
<Sheet Open="_overflow" Title="@Title" Subtitle="Actions" TestId="pagehead-overflow-sheet"
       OnClose="() => _overflow = false">
    <div class="a-stack" @onclick="() => _overflow = false">
        @Secondary
    </div>
</Sheet>

@code {
    /// <summary>The section this screen belongs to, shown above the title.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? Eyebrow { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Where the eyebrow goes, when it is a way back rather than a label.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? EyebrowHref { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The thing this screen is about.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public string Title { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether the title is an identifier rather than prose.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// An entity is called <c>work_orders</c> in the descriptor, the URL and the API, so the screen
    /// spells it the way the operator will have to type it.
    /// </remarks>
    [Parameter]
    public bool Mono { get; set; }

    /// <summary>One line under the title.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? Subtitle { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The one action this screen is for — the button that stays on the row at every width.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// A screen may have none: an entity with no unapplied edit has nothing to preview, and an
    /// empty slot is right where a disabled button would be a control whose only outcome is
    /// nothing.
    /// </remarks>
    [Parameter]
    public RenderFragment? Primary { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The rest of the screen's controls — shown beside the primary one, or behind the overflow
    /// menu on a phone.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// <b>Separate from <see cref="Primary"/> rather than ordered within one slot</b>, because
    /// "which of these is the action" is the screen's answer and not a position this component
    /// could infer. One slot of buttons is what produced the wall this splits up.
    /// </remarks>
    [Parameter]
    public RenderFragment? Secondary { get; set; }

    private bool _overflow;
}
